    "note": "Not a match, and not expected to be one. Our toy counts every interaction above 60 TeV of neutrino energy in a full 1 km^3 (46/yr). IceCube's own public HESE simulation, for the same flux, keeps 7.0/yr astrophysical events (about 1 in 7). Per energy bin it keeps 0.08 of our count at 60-110 TeV, rising to about 0.41 above 1 PeV: the outer layers are the veto, not the counting volume, and below a few hundred TeV much of the energy leaves (muon tracks, neutral currents) so the deposited energy falls under the 60 TeV cut. The observed 8.3/yr also contains atmospheric neutrinos and muons."
